[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: laptop0
diagnosis_text:
Router0’s VLAN 20 gateway interface is not down. On router0, the sub-interface eth0.20 is present and operational: “ip -br a” shows eth0.20 UP/LOWER_UP with 172.16.20.1/24, and router0 can ping 172.16.20.10 successfully. From laptop0, a direct ping to 172.16.20.1 also succeeds, proving L2 for VLAN 20 and the gateway SVI are healthy. The only router0 interfaces currently DOWN are eth1, eth3, and eth5, which are unrelated to VLAN 20.

The observed symptom (host can reach same-VLAN peers but nothing outside) is explained by a host default-gateway misconfiguration. On laptop0, “ip route” shows the default via 172.16.20.99, not 172.16.20.1. Its ARP table shows 172.16.20.99 as incomplete while 172.16.20.1 resolves to the router’s MAC. Consequently, any off-subnet traffic is sent to a nonexistent next hop, never leaving the VLAN, while intra-VLAN traffic (including the gateway IP itself) works.

Localization: laptop0, routing table/default route. The gateway router’s VLAN-20 sub-interface (router0 eth0.20) is UP and not the cause. Root cause: laptop0’s default gateway is set to 172.16.20.99, which is not a valid router on VLAN 20.

Corrective fix: Reconfigure laptop0’s default route to point to 172.16.20.1 (or renew DHCP to acquire the correct gateway), clear any stale ARP if needed, and retest external reachability. No changes are required on router0; its VLAN-20 sub-interface (eth0.20) is up and functioning.
=== END ===